This version differs from my earlier sketch in one way: the order insert, the stored response, and the key's `completed` status all commit in a single transaction. That makes recovery safe. If the server crashes mid-request, Postgres rolls back the order, so letting a retry take over the abandoned key can't create a duplicate.

## Table definition

```sql
CREATE TABLE idempotency_keys (
  user_id       text        NOT NULL,
  key           text        NOT NULL,
  request_hash  text        NOT NULL,
  status        text        NOT NULL CHECK (status IN ('in_progress', 'completed')),
  attempt       int         NOT NULL DEFAULT 1,       -- fencing token, bumped on takeover
  locked_at     timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),   -- when the current attempt started
  response_code int,
  response_body jsonb,
  created_at    timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
  completed_at  timestamptz,
  PRIMARY KEY (user_id, key),
  CHECK (status <> 'completed' OR response_code IS NOT NULL)
);

CREATE INDEX idempotency_keys_created_at_idx ON idempotency_keys (created_at);
```

