I don't know which paging tool you use, so I've assumed PagerDuty and written this as Terraform. The same structure maps to Opsgenie or Grafana OnCall.

```hcl
terraform {
  required_providers {
    pagerduty = { source = "PagerDuty/pagerduty" }
  }
}

variable "user_ids" {
  description = "PagerDuty user IDs in rota order (same order as config.json)"
  type        = list(string)
  validation {
    condition     = length(var.user_ids) >= 3
    error_message = "Need at least 3 engineers so primary and backup rotate sanely."
  }
}

variable "manager_user_id" {
  description = "Final escalation target if both primary and backup miss the page"
  type        = string
}

variable "time_zone" {
  type    = string
  default = "Europe/London"
}

variable "rota_start" {
  description = "First handover, RFC 3339 with offset"
  type        = string
  default     = "2025-07-07T10:00:00+01:00"
}

variable "escalation_minutes" {
  type    = number
  default = 10
}

locals {
  n = length(var.user_ids)
  # Backup rotation is the primary list shifted by one, so the backup is
  # normally next week's primary (matches rota.py when nobody is on leave).
  backup_ids = concat(slice(var.user_ids, 1, local.n), slice(var.user_ids, 0, 1))
}

resource "pagerduty_schedule" "primary" {
  name      = "On-call: primary"
  time_zone = var.time_zone

  layer {
    name                         = "Weekly primary"
    start                        = var.rota_start
    rotation_virtual_start       = var.rota_start
    rotation_turn_length_seconds = 604800 # 7 days
    users                        = var.user_ids
  }
}

resource "pagerduty_schedule" "backup" {
  name      = "On-call: backup"
  time_zone = var.time_zone

  layer {
    name                         = "Weekly backup"
    start                        = var.rota_start
    rotation_virtual_start       = var.rota_start
    rotation_turn_length_seconds = 604800
    users                        = local.backup_ids
  }
}

resource "pagerduty_escalation_policy" "oncall" {
  name      = "On-call escalation"
  num_loops = 2 # repeat the whole chain twice before giving up

  # Level 1: primary. If unacknowledged after N minutes, go to level 2.
  rule {
    escalation_delay_in_minutes = var.escalation_minutes
    target {
      type = "schedule_reference"
      id   = pagerduty_schedule.primary.id
    }
  }

  # Level 2: backup, paged at +10 min.
  rule {
    escalation_delay_in_minutes = var.escalation_minutes
    target {
      type = "schedule_reference"
      id   = pagerduty_schedule.backup.id
    }
  }

  # Level 3: manager, paged at +20 min if neither has acknowledged.
  rule {
    escalation_delay_in_minutes = var.escalation_minutes
    target {
      type = "user_reference"
      id   = var.manager_user_id
    }
  }
}

resource "pagerduty_service" "main" {
  name                    = "Production"
  escalation_policy       = pagerduty_escalation_policy.oncall.id
  alert_creation          = "create_alerts_and_incidents"
  acknowledgement_timeout = 1800 # unacknowledged-after-ack re-triggers after 30 min
  auto_resolve_timeout    = "null"
}
```

**Timeline when nobody responds**

| Time | Who is paged |
|---|---|
| 0 min | Primary |
| 10 min | Backup (primary is still being paged) |
| 20 min | Manager |
| 30 min | Chain repeats from the primary (`num_loops = 2`) |

**Things to know**
- **I added a third level.** If the primary missed a page, the backup could too, and a policy that ends at the backup fails silently. Drop the manager rule if you don't want it.
- **Leave isn't handled here.** The schedules use a plain rotation, so they match `rota.py` only when nobody is away. For leave and swaps, apply the generated rota as schedule overrides (the sync step I offered earlier), or the schedule and the calendar export will disagree.
- **Keep the shifted-list trick in mind if you add engineers.** Changing `user_ids` reshuffles who is primary and backup from the next turn, so change it right after a handover.

**Check the cause of the two misses**

A backup only helps if the primary's page was delivered and not noticed. It won't help if the alert never fired, was routed to the wrong service, or reached the engineer through a muted push notification. Look at the timelines of the two missed incidents in your paging tool. If the pages were delivered but unanswered, this policy fits. If not, fix that first. Either way, check that each engineer's high-urgency notification rules include a phone call or SMS immediately, repeated every few minutes. That setting is per user, outside this Terraform, and it often matters more than the escalation structure.

